WEBVTT

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:07.990
I originally made Petri Palette for the

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:09.730
students that are in maybe middle school

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:11.260
or high school that always felt that

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.970
science wasn't for them.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.350
I used to be like this a long time ago

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:18.080
when I saw that science maybe wasn't for

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.220
me because I felt that I wasn't smart

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.020
enough.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:23.190
And I've tried to convince them through

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.630
making my art that really it's not all

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:27.610
that they had put it on the mighty

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:30.640
pedestal to be and that really it's for

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:31.760
everybody.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:37.010
Biology is often seen as hard because it

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:39.740
then gets down to the microscopic level

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.300
of it where it's seen as memorization

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:45.920
rather than application in the real world.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.060
Well, I'd always loved both of them but I

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:54.050
guess I saw in ninth grade I was able to

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:55.950
present some of my art at the Museum of

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:56.710
Fine Arts

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:58.960
and I realized that really art is no

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.780
different than biology because art's

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:05.470
purpose is to capture and recreate life

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:09.190
itself therefore they are kind of one and

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:10.440
the same.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.020
I was able to spend six weeks studying

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.040
metastatic melanoma in the Cancer

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:20.490
Institute.

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:23.500
It was incredible, as I got to be able to

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.830
see how cancer cells or HeLa cells

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:29.110
actually looked in real life and how

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:31.840
they're not quite how they look in a

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.670
textbook but being able to experience and

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:39.520
experiment them in the lab was life

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.200
changing.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:47.750
I typically try to feel out how the

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:50.860
abstract view of it would be.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:55.090
For example, I think of interphase, part of

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:59.300
cellular mitosis as kind of stringy and

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:02.100
wind it up in a ball of yarn kind of way

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.380
and I try to capture the essence of that

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:08.410
cellular activity and then I make a little

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:10.570
card that has what happens in that

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:14.650
activity in a brief little synopsis.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:20.700
My thought was that they would learn of

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.590
these biological activities

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:25.620
and that they aren't just, you know,

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:28.390
conceptual and abstract but they really do

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:30.630
happen. This is what they look like. It's

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:33.380
not just the words on a page. Maybe it

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:36.370
helps them in the classroom.

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:41.010
I really hope that they are able to

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:43.770
understand better that this impacts their

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.650
view of biology and the sciences and that

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:49.350
maybe this influences them into maybe

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:52.140
becoming a scientist or working in the

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.300
medical field of some sort.
